%I A401080 #39 Oct 27 2026 16:50:00
%S A401080 16,21,56,78,120,153,221
%N A401080 Numbers k such that binomial(k,2) equals another nontrivial binomial coefficient.
%C A401080 We do not know if this sequence contains an infinite number of terms, nor even if others exist.
%C A401080 It is remarkable that all known nontrivial equalities between binomial coefficients involve a coefficient of the form binomial(k,2), except for equality binomial(104,39) = binomial(103,40).
%C A401080 a(8), if it exists, exceeds 5000. - _Sean A. Irvine_, Oct 20 2026
%C A401080 From _Pontus von Brömssen_, Oct 20 2026: (Start)
%C A401080 If k is a term, binomial(k,2) is a term of A003015.
%C A401080 Since A003015(9) is not of the form binomial(k,2) and A003015(10) (if it exists) is greater than 10^62 (see comment by _Michael Ramirez_), a(8) (if it exists) is greater than sqrt(2*10^62) = 1.414...*10^31.
%C A401080 (End)
%H A401080 Benjamin M.M. de Weger, <a href="https://repub.eur.nl/pub/1356/1356_ps.pdf">Equal binomial coefficients: some elementary considerations</a>, August 1995.
%H A401080 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singmaster%27s_conjecture">Singmaster's conjecture</a>
%e A401080 binomial(16,2) = binomial(10,3) = 120.
%e A401080 binomial(21,2) = binomial(10,4) = 210.
%e A401080 binomial(56,2) = binomial(22,3) = 1540.
%e A401080 binomial(78,2) = binomial(15,5) = binomial(14,6) = 3003.
%e A401080 binomial(120,2) = binomial(36,3) = 7140.
%e A401080 binomial(153,2) = binomial(19,5) = 11628.
%e A401080 binomial(221,2) = binomial(17,8) = 24310.
%Y A401080 Cf. A007318, A098565, A003015, A161680.
%K A401080 nonn,more,new
%O A401080 1,1
%A A401080 _Charles Delaporte_, Oct 14 2026
